      "text": "To solve for the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we start by recalling a fundamental property of matrix inverses: the inverse of a product of matrices is the product of their inverses in reverse order. That is,\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) and then compute its inverse.\n\nFirst, we need to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\). By definition, \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}\\). To find \\(\\mathbf{A}\\), we need to determine \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). However, since we are given \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\), we can find \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) using the relationship \\(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix. But it is more straightforward to use the given \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) directly to find \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A} = (\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nPerforming the matrix multiplication,\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find its inverse. The inverse of a 2x2 matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we have \\(a = -1\\), \\(b = -5\\), \\(c = 1\\), and \\(d = 4\\). The determinant \\(ad - bc\\) is\n\n\\[\n(-1)(4) - (-5)(1) = -4 + 5 = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1} \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
