      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let's denote the invertible matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ by $\\mathbf{A}$. We are given that $\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nFirst, we need to find the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2$. The inverse of a product of matrices is the product of their inverses in reverse order. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\n\\]\nGiven that $\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we need to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}$.\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}$, we multiply the matrix $\\mathbf{A}^{-1}$ by itself:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nWe perform the matrix multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThus, the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2$ is:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
