      "text": "To find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we can use the property of inverses that states \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2\\). This can be computed by multiplying \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) by itself.\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2 = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nLet's perform the matrix multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo compute this, we perform the following steps:\n\n1. The element at position \\((1,1)\\):\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) = 4 - 5 = -1\n\\]\n\n2. The element at position \\((1,2)\\):\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) = 10 - 15 = -5\n\\]\n\n3. The element at position \\((2,1)\\):\n\\[\n-1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) = -2 + 3 = 1\n\\]\n\n4. The element at position \\((2,2)\\):\n\\[\n-1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) = -5 + 9 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{A}^{-1})^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
